WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.250
Some movies are such duds that they don't
just lose a studio millions, they also put

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.160
entire companies out of business.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.900
Here's a look back at some historic mega-bombs
that straight up bankrupted studios.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.140
Riding parent company Warner Bros' coattails
- and the success that came with the Harry

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:21.699
Potter films - New Line Cinema wanted another
massive fantasy hit following their extremely

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.160
lucrative and award-winning Lord of the Rings
trilogy.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:29.059
So, it put a version of The Golden Compass,
author Philip Pullman's first entry in the

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.600
His Dark Materials trilogy, into production.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:36.980
New Line pumped $200 million into the project,
more than it had spent on anything - including

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:37.980
The Lord of the Rings.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.800
To offset production costs, the company pre-sold
the overseas rights to Compass, meaning that

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.820
when the film hit theaters outside of North
America, it wouldn't recoup a dime.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:52.280
That made profit virtually impossible… as
did the film's relatively small $70 million

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.480
domestic take.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.699
This fiasco was the main reason Warner Bros.
absorbed New Line into its existing film production

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.040
divisions in February 2008, ousting chief
Bob Shaye and 90 percent of New Line's employees.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.020
As one of the most popular sitcoms of the
2000s, the American version of The Office

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:14.280
led to movie careers for several of its stars,
notably Steve Carell, John Krasinski, and

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:14.920
Ed Helms.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.040
While Rainn Wilson, who portrayed Dunder Mifflin
weirdo beet farmer Dwight Schrute, has graced

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.369
the big screen plenty of times in his own
right, he arguably hasn't had the same mainstream

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:26.200
appeal as his Office co-stars.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.680
Wilson's first shot at leading man status
came courtesy of the 2008 comedy The Rocker.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.560
Despite the stellar cast - which also included
Emma Stone and Teddy Geiger as Wilson's young

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.890
bandmates - audiences didn't think The Rocker
rocked.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:44.270
It grossed just $2.6 million over its opening
weekend, at the time the worst performance

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.140
ever for a widely released movie.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.079
It quickly disappeared from theaters, and
soon thereafter, so did the division of Fox

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.320
that released the film.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:56.440
Fox Atomic had been created in 2007 to bring
out cool comedies and edgy horror movies.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:02.000
In early 2009, thanks in part to the spectacular
failure of The Rocker, Fox shut down the group.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.800
"How dare you!"

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.100
"Excuse me?"

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.580
"How dare you call yourself a musician."

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.910
In 1994, Carolco Pictures had two projects
in the works: the pirate adventure Cutthroat

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.720
Island and the Arnold Schwarzenegger epic
Crusade.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.740
Short on funds, Carolco decided to not waste
another penny on Crusade, essentially throwing

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:27.370
away the $13 million it had already spent
on the movie, placing all its eggs in one

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.330
basket - the pirate movie.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:33.840
A foolhardy move for sure - the only successful
pirate movie in the previous decade or so

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:34.340
was Hook.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.180
But Carolco thought that this genre would
be just the thing to return the company to

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.540
financial solvency.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.040
On screen, Cutthroat Island looked like a
million bucks… which is about one percent

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.120
of how much it cost to make the movie and
a tenth of what it earned.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:52.260
Carolco spent an astounding $98 million on
Cutthroat Island, only to watch the movie

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.080
sink at the box office with a $10 million
haul.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.560
The studio ultimately had no faith in its
expensive last gasp - the company filed for

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.200
bankruptcy in November 1995, six weeks before
Cutthroat Island hit theaters.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.060
From Tekken to Postal to Super Mario Bros.,
movies based on video games are almost categorically

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:10.600
awful.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.880
One of the better ones, according to critics?

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.500
Final Fantasy: The Spirits Within.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:18.620
One of the first computer-animated movies,
it's an absolutely stunning piece of work

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.090
and a technical achievement, as it's the first
movie to render everything digitally, including

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.260
largely realistic human characters.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.360
Starring the substantial voice talents of
Ming-Na Wen, Alec Baldwin, and Ving Rhames,

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:35.070
The Spirits Within is based on a long-running,
thoughtful, and mystical series of video games.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:40.370
With 142 million units sold overall, Final
Fantasy is among the best-selling game franchises

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.700
ever, but that's still a small cross-section
of the moviegoing public.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:48.220
The film apparently lacked crossover appeal,
because despite all that amazing animation,

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.620
Final Fantasy brought in a weak $32 million
in North America.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.240
That wouldn't be so terrible if filmmakers
hadn't spent $137 million to bring it to the

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:56.740
screen.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:01.340
The film was the first ever produced by Square
Pictures, an offshoot of gaming company Square.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.720
It was so expensive that it would also be
the division's last, as the parent company

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.700
soon absorbed it into the newly formed Square
Enix.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.960
One of the biggest emerging markets for movies
is China, and Global Road set out to get in

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.989
on that potential box office while also making
movies that North American filmgoers might

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:18.480
enjoy.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.780
It failed both segments.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:24.860
Desperately in debt to creditors like Viacom,
Disney, and NBCUniversal in the summer of

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:29.419
2018, Global Road started laying off staff
and selling the distribution rights to movies

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.400
not yet released.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:34.710
One of those unreleased films was City of
Lies, about the murder investigation of Notorious

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:35.340
B.I.G.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.400
City of Lies certainly couldn't earn any money
sitting in a vault somewhere, but the movies

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.540
Global Road did release were financially disastrous.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.220
Despite a cast that included Jeff Goldblum,
Dave Baustista, and Jodie Foster, the weird

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.500
sci-fi drama Hotel Artemis grossed just $6.7
million domestic.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.220
Another major release, the family film Show
Dogs, caused some controversy and cost the

00:04:56.229 --> 00:05:00.639
studio a lot of money when it had to be re-cut
to eliminate some inappropriate scenes about

00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:03.759
a dog getting its, ahem, privates fondled.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:05.700
Surprise, surprise - it still bombed.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:10.640
"You waited for me to get back here
for that, didn't you?"

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:11.200
“Haha”

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.720
Israel-based Bank Leumi, which had fronted
Global Road a lot of City of Lies' costs,

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.520
filed suit when the studio defaulted on a
$10.8 million payment.

00:05:20.789 --> 00:05:26.400
In September 2018, Global Road filed for bankruptcy
after a rough year didn't produce the mega-successful

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.360
hits it needed to stay afloat.

